---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Buying Gift Cards With Crypto in 2026: The Real Options - Mobazha"
  description="Paxful and NoOnes are gone. Where to buy or sell gift cards with bitcoin now, and how to do it without handing your money to a stranger."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-[var(--color-text-primary)] sm:text-5xl">
        Buying gift cards with crypto: what is actually left
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Two years ago this was a crowded market. Today it is not. Two of the biggest platforms are gone, and the ones that remain do very different things.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        This page separates them, because "buy gift cards with crypto" covers two completely different activities.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What changed</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>Paxful</strong> shut down in 2025 after a US enforcement action. It had processed over $6 billion and served around 10 million users.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>NoOnes</strong>, which took in Paxful's users, wound down in August 2026 after EU sanctions cut off its partners. It had 2.5 million users.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Both were peer-to-peer platforms. Both let people trade gift cards for crypto. Both are gone.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">Category one: buying new cards from a shop</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        You pay crypto, you get a code. No counterparty, no negotiation, no risk of a stolen card.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>Bitrefill</strong> is the largest. It has sold more than 16 million gift cards for crypto and covers most countries. <strong>Coinsbee</strong> lists over 5,000 brands across 185 countries and accepts 250+ coins. <strong>TheBitcoinCompany</strong> focuses on gift cards and prepaid Visa and Mastercard, with discounts up to 50% on some brands.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you want a card right now, at face value or close to it, use one of these. This is the safe path and there is no reason to complicate it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">Category two: trading with another person</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        This is where the old platforms lived. You have a card, someone else has crypto, and you agree on a rate. Usually the card sells below face value, because the buyer is taking on risk.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        That market still exists, but it has moved. Some of it is on community forums, some on Telegram, some with local agents. The rules matter more than the platform.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Community trading groups often ban resale and account trading outright, and they treat unsolicited messages as a scam signal. Read the rules before you post. Telegram groups have no rules at all, and that is the problem with them.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">Where Mobazha fits</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Mobazha is not a gift card shop. It is a marketplace where people trade with each other, and gift cards are one category among several.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you are selling a card you already own, this is the structure you want. If you just want to buy an Amazon card at face value, use Bitrefill.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What else you can sell here</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Gift cards get the search traffic, but they are not the whole platform. On Mobazha you can list:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <li><strong>Physical goods.</strong> New items, stock you already hold, or products sourced from suppliers who store and ship for you.</li>
        <li><strong>Second-hand items.</strong> The camera you stopped using, the jacket you never wear, the equipment taking up space.</li>
        <li><strong>Digital goods.</strong> Accounts you own, software, subscriptions, game items, templates, designs, ebooks.</li>
        <li><strong>Things you make.</strong> Handmade goods, art, custom work, physical or digital.</li>
        <li><strong>Services.</strong> A skill, a piece of work, a small job.</li>
        <li><strong>Gift cards.</strong> Cards you bought and no longer need, sold to another person at a rate you both agree on.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        There is no category menu to fight with and no algorithm deciding your listing does not fit. If selling it is legal where you are, it can be listed.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">How the money is held</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        This is the part that matters when you are trading with a stranger.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Payments go into a 2-of-3 multisig address. The buyer, the seller and a neutral third party each hold one key. Nobody can move the money alone, including Mobazha.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If the two sides disagree, an independent arbitrator decides. Arbitrators set their own fees and languages, and they never touch shipping or the funds.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        There is no KYC, no platform balance, and no account to fund. You pay from your own wallet, and the seller receives to theirs.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">Who this is for</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        People who already hold crypto and want to spend it. Sellers in countries where getting paid across a border is slow or unreliable. Anyone who has had a store closed by an automated decision and lost the inventory along with it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">A safety rule worth remembering</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Normal discounted gift cards trade at 85% to 90% of face value. Below 70% is not a good deal, it is a warning. Cards at that price are usually bought with stolen payment methods, and when the card gets clawed back, you are the one holding a dead code.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        No platform can protect you from that if you ignore it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">How to start</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you are buying a card: use a shop. If you are selling anything at all: open a store, list the item with a real description and a real price, and let the escrow do the rest.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8">
        <a class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-brand-light)] underline" href="https://app.mobazha.org/">Open a store on Mobazha</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
